      boxedWarning: "BOXED WARNING: use only in hospital or ambulatory settings with continuous respiratory and cardiac monitoring, immediate resuscitative drugs, age/size-appropriate bag-mask and intubation equipment, and personnel skilled in airway management. A dedicated observer must continuously monitor deeply sedated patients. Apnea, hypoventilation, laryngospasm and cardiorespiratory arrest can occur.",
      adverseEffects: ["Apnea, hypoventilation, laryngospasm, hypotension, tachycardia, injection injury, hiccups, cough, skeletal movement/tremor, seizure-like activity, postoperative drowsiness, nausea/vomiting and shivering."],
      contraindications: ["Latent or manifest porphyria, barbiturate hypersensitivity, or a situation in which general anesthesia is contraindicated. Use extreme caution with severe respiratory, cardiovascular, hepatic, renal or endocrine impairment and status asthmaticus."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Confirm fasting/aspiration plan, porphyria history, airway equipment, continuous ECG/oxygenation/ventilation and a separate analgesia plan before use.", "For outpatient procedures, ensure recovery criteria and an escort; patients should not drive or perform hazardous tasks for the label-specified interval. For ECT, document seizure quality and hemodynamic recovery."],
      interactions: ["Opioids, benzodiazepines, alcohol and other CNS depressants amplify apnea and hypotension. Chronic enzyme inducers or tolerance can alter dose response. Other seizure-threshold-changing drugs affect ECT interpretation."],
      keyLabs: ["Continuous ECG, blood pressure, oxygenation and ventilation", "Neurologic and seizure-quality assessment for ECT", "Hepatic/renal context and pregnancy testing when clinically relevant"],
      nclexTraps: ["Methohexital is hypnosis, not analgesia.", "Its rapid first recovery reflects redistribution and does not erase respiratory risk.", "Porphyria is a true contraindication, not a minor precaution."],
      populationRisks: anesthesiaPopulationRisks("Label route and age differ: IV use is for adults, while selected rectal/IM induction labeling exists for pediatric patients older than one month; verify product instructions.", "Frailty and reduced reserve increase cardiorespiratory depression; start at the low end under anesthesia direction."),
      sourceNote: "Curated from current U.S. methohexital labeling: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=f76d4ab9-2f6c-420b-afec-b9cdfdc083ce",
