      usedToTreat: "Historical and selected special-access induction of general anesthesia and specialist reduction of cerebral metabolic demand or refractory seizures; U.S. routine availability is limited, so local formulary status must be verified.",
      description: "Thiopental is a highly lipid-soluble thiobarbiturate that produces unconsciousness within one circulation time. A single dose wears off mainly by redistribution from brain to muscle and fat, but repeated doses saturate peripheral compartments and prolong recovery. It lowers cerebral metabolic rate, blood flow and intracranial pressure, yet can cause profound apnea, hypotension and tissue injury if extravasated or injected intra-arterially. It provides no analgesia.",
      mechanism: "Thiopental prolongs GABA-A chloride-channel opening and suppresses excitatory neuronal signaling. Rapid brain entry explains induction speed; redistribution explains early awakening; slow hepatic metabolism and fat storage explain accumulation. Reduced cerebral neuronal activity lowers oxygen consumption and cerebral blood flow. Like other barbiturates, it induces ALA synthase and can precipitate acute porphyria.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Treat as a potent general anesthetic requiring continuous airway, ventilation and circulatory monitoring with immediate resuscitation capability. Avoid in acute intermittent or other acute porphyrias. Severe extravasation can cause tissue necrosis and accidental intra-arterial injection can cause vasospasm, thrombosis and limb ischemia. Verify current product source and institutional protocol because routine U.S. availability is limited."),
      adverseEffects: ["Apnea, respiratory depression, laryngospasm/bronchospasm, hypotension, myocardial depression, arrhythmia, prolonged sedation after repeated doses, injection pain, extravasation necrosis, intra-arterial ischemia, nausea and emergence effects."],
