      description: "Pyridostigmine reversibly inhibits acetylcholinesterase, increasing acetylcholine at nicotinic neuromuscular and muscarinic autonomic synapses. In myasthenia gravis, more acetylcholine improves transmission across a reduced functional receptor population. Too much produces cholinergic toxicity and can paradoxically worsen weakness. Its quaternary ammonium structure limits entry into the brain, so peripheral secretions, diarrhea, bradycardia, fasciculation and weakness dominate.",
      mechanism: "Carbamylation of acetylcholinesterase temporarily prevents acetylcholine hydrolysis. At the motor end plate, repeated transmitter-receptor opportunities improve the safety factor of neuromuscular transmission. At muscarinic organs, the same acetylcholine increase causes salivation, lacrimation, bronchial secretions, bronchospasm, bradycardia, abdominal cramping, diarrhea, urination and miosis. Renal elimination makes toxicity more likely when kidney function declines.",
      boxedWarning: "BOXED WARNING applies to soman nerve-agent pretreatment labeling: pyridostigmine alone does not protect after exposure and must be stopped at the first poisoning signs; atropine and pralidoxime must then be used promptly, and protective garments remain primary protection. This military indication must not be confused with routine myasthenia gravis dosing. Across indications, excessive dosing can create a cholinergic crisis with secretions, bradycardia and worsening weakness.",
      adverseEffects: ["Nausea, cramping, diarrhea, salivation, sweating, miosis, urinary urgency, bronchial secretions/bronchospasm, bradycardia, fasciculation, muscle cramps and paradoxical weakness from cholinergic excess."],
