      interactions: ["Antimuscarinics reduce muscarinic symptoms but do not reverse nicotinic weakness. Aminoglycosides, magnesium, fluoroquinolones, certain beta blockers and other neuromuscular-interfering drugs may worsen myasthenia. Other cholinesterase inhibitors increase cholinergic toxicity."],
      keyLabs: ["Respiratory rate/effort, vital capacity or negative inspiratory force when crisis is possible", "Heart rate, secretions, bowel/bladder symptoms and strength trajectory", "Renal function for dose accumulation"],
      nclexTraps: ["Both too little effective transmission and too much acetylcholine can produce weakness; secretions, diarrhea, miosis and bradycardia point toward cholinergic excess.", "The soman boxed warning describes pretreatment, not stand-alone treatment after exposure.", "Pyridostigmine has limited CNS penetration because it is a quaternary compound."],
      populationRisks: anesthesiaPopulationRisks("Pediatric myasthenia and reversal use require weight-based specialist direction; nerve-agent labeling has its own age/operational limits.", "Renal clearance falls with age, increasing cholinergic toxicity and bradycardia risk."),
      sourceNote: "Curated from current U.S. pyridostigmine labeling, including the indication-specific boxed warning: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=0b296555-c32f-42db-b5f3-ea981a3dd2f8",
      tags: ["frontier-wave4", "pyridostigmine", "Mestinon", "acetylcholinesterase", "myasthenia gravis", "cholinergic crisis", "soman"]
