      generic: "glycopyrrolate",
      aliases: ["Robinul", "glycopyrronium", "quaternary antimuscarinic"],
      brandExamples: ["Robinul"],
      class: "Peripheral quaternary muscarinic antagonist antisecretory and vagolytic",
      usedToTreat: "Reduction of salivary/airway secretions and vagal reflexes during anesthesia, protection from muscarinic effects when neostigmine or pyridostigmine reverses nondepolarizing blockade, and formulation-specific treatment of sialorrhea, COPD or other indications.",
      description: "Glycopyrrolate is a quaternary antimuscarinic that blocks acetylcholine at peripheral muscarinic receptors with little blood-brain-barrier penetration. In anesthesia it dries secretions, counters vagal bradycardia and is paired with neostigmine so increased acetylcholine can reverse skeletal blockade without causing dangerous bradycardia, bronchial secretions and bowel activity. Its limited CNS entry distinguishes it from atropine, but peripheral anticholinergic toxicity remains substantial.",
      mechanism: "Competitive antagonism at M1-M5 muscarinic receptors reduces glandular secretion, slows GI/bladder smooth-muscle activity, inhibits vagal M2 effects on the heart and causes mydriatic/ocular effects if exposure reaches the eye. The permanently charged quaternary ammonium structure limits passive CNS diffusion. It does not block nicotinic receptors, so it cannot reverse paralysis by itself.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Tachycardia, acute angle closure, heat illness from impaired sweating, urinary retention, ileus and thickened respiratory secretions can be serious. Pairing with a cholinesterase inhibitor must use the correct ratio and timing; glycopyrrolate treats muscarinic effects but does not restore neuromuscular transmission."),
      adverseEffects: ["Dry mouth, thick secretions, blurred vision/mydriasis, tachycardia, urinary retention, constipation/ileus, reduced sweating and heat intolerance, confusion rarely, and injection-site reactions."],
