      nursingEssentials: ["Verify indication and formulation. During reversal, administer with the ordered neostigmine or pyridostigmine ratio and continue train-of-four, airway, heart-rate and secretion monitoring.", "Assess urinary output/retention, bowel sounds/distention, glaucoma symptoms, temperature and ability to clear thick secretions. Provide oral care and avoid overheating."],
      interactions: ["Other anticholinergics, antihistamines, tricyclics, antipsychotics and antiparkinsonian agents add urinary retention, ileus, tachycardia, blurred vision and heat risk. Potassium chloride solid oral dosage forms can worsen GI injury when motility is reduced."],
      keyLabs: ["Heart rate/rhythm, blood pressure, respiratory secretion/airway assessment", "Urine output and bladder/bowel assessment", "Train-of-four and ventilatory recovery when paired with reversal therapy"],
      nclexTraps: ["Glycopyrrolate blocks muscarinic side effects; it does not reverse nicotinic skeletal paralysis.", "Limited CNS penetration does not mean no anticholinergic toxicity.", "Drying secretions can make mucus harder to clear."],
      populationRisks: anesthesiaPopulationRisks("Infants and small children are sensitive to hyperthermia, thick secretions and formulation excipients; use exact weight-based instructions.", "Older adults have higher urinary-retention, constipation/ileus, glaucoma and tachyarrhythmia risk."),
      sourceNote: "Curated from current U.S. glycopyrrolate injection labeling: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=d670bd26-8a44-44be-90e1-107d4c380233&version=6",
