      description: "Sevoflurane is a nonpungent volatile general anesthetic that suppresses cortical and spinal neural networks to produce unconsciousness and immobility; low blood-gas solubility lets inspired concentration change brain level and anesthetic depth quickly. Its airway profile makes it useful for inhaled pediatric induction. Dose-dependent vasodilation and respiratory depression lower blood pressure and ventilation. It can trigger malignant hyperthermia and reacts with carbon-dioxide absorbents to form Compound A, making fresh-gas-flow and absorber management clinically relevant.",
      mechanism: "Sevoflurane changes activity across GABA-A, glycine, potassium, nicotinic and other neural targets, suppressing cortical integration and spinal movement. Low blood solubility limits uptake into blood, speeding equilibration and recovery. Metabolism by CYP2E1 releases inorganic fluoride, while base-containing CO2 absorbents degrade it to fluoromethyl compounds including Compound A; exposure rises with low flows, higher concentration, heat and certain absorbents.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Contraindicated in known or suspected malignant-hyperthermia susceptibility. Rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia, rigidity, acidosis, hyperkalemia or temperature requires immediate trigger discontinuation, 100% oxygen and dantrolene. Follow product fresh-gas-flow limits to minimize Compound A renal risk, monitor QT in susceptible patients, and replace desiccated absorbent because exothermic degradation can overheat the circuit."),
